  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (Through next Monday)...\n\nSmoke Impacts: Wildfire smoke will continue to impact our region \nfor the foreseeable future, keeping skies quite hazy, with reduced\nsurface visibilities and poor air quality at times.\n\nThere is a slight risk of fog developing by sunrise across\nportions of Fallon and Carter counties as boundary level moisture \nsurges overnight along the Dakota/Montana borders. Any fog should\nlift by 9 AM.  \n\nConditions will remain warm & mostly dry today as zonal flow\ncontinues over our region on top of a large dome of high pressure \nanchored over the Four-Corners region. Low level moisture will \nremain elevated in the far east. Once again, the environment \ncould favor a strong t-storm or two along the Dakota/MT border \nlate in the day if the midlevel cap breaks (15% chance). \nOtherwise, it will be dry with temps in the upper 80s and lower \n90s. Winds won't be strong, but N-NE winds in the afternoon may \nsee local gusts ~20 mph. This will keep fire weather conditions \nelevated, especially in the western half of the CWA where RH \nreadings will bottom in the lower to mid teens this afternoon.\n\nThe entire forecast area begins to turn cooler and more humid on \nWednesday, as heights fall in response to a trough dropping out \nof southwest Canada into the Pacific NW. This will induce \nstronger and deeper easterly flow advecting higher dewpoints \nwestward across our CWA, while Pacific and monsoonal moisture move\nover the region from the south/southwest. The potential for \nshower and t-storms dramatically increases by late Wednesday over\nthe east and spreads west into the evening.  \n\nBy Thursday and Friday, ensembles agree PWATs will reach up to \n1.25\" as far west as Billings. Progs indicate a shortwave tracking\nover the region as well. Thus, we expect a period of showers \nand embedded t-storms capable of producing heavy rain over the \narea. WPC has our area highlighted with a marginal to slight risk \nof excessive rain (flooding risks) Thursday. Local burn scars will\nbe at an elevated risk of seeing flash flooding. \n\nRegarding fire weather...the onset of increased lightning \nactivity may result in new fire starts, as almost our entire \nforecast area has not seen measurable rain yet in August. However, \nheavy rain with the storms and higher RH readings will hopefully \nmitigate fire starts. \n\nWeekend...Heights begin to rise over our region this weekend in \nresponse to another Pacific low developing off the west coast. \nLook for temps warming up by Sunday, and while the chance of dail\nthunderstorms continues, we should see less overall coverage of \nstorms. \n\nLook for temperatures to cool to the 80s Wednesday then 70s\nThursday and Friday. Readings will begin to gradually warm this\nweekend, reaching lower to mid 80s by Sunday, and 80s to around 90\nfor Monday. BT",
